+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface (v2)
+
+**Feature**: `002-redact-phones-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-14
+
+This document **supersedes**
+[`../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/cli-contract.md`](../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/cli-contract.md)
+as the authoritative description of the CLI. Two things changed, both requested by the user and
+both deliberate amendments to what that document declared stable:
+
+1. The `--count` summary gains a fourth key, `phones` (FR-037).
+2. A new option, `--out-dir DIR`, adds a second output destination (FR-040).
+
+Everything else it said still holds. The redaction rules are in
+[`redaction-rules.md`](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires Python 3.11+ with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No installation step, no third-party package.
+
+### Arguments
+
+| Argument | Cardinality | Meaning |
+|----------|-------------|---------|
+| `--count` | 0 or 1 | Print a redaction summary to stderr after all output. |
+| `--out-dir DIR` | 0 or 1 | Write each FILE's redacted lines to `DIR/<FILE's name>` instead of to stdout. Requires at least one FILE. |
+| `FILE` | 0 or more | Input file to read, in the order given. |
+
+`--count`, `--out-dir`, and FILE arguments may be interleaved in any order: `--out-dir d --count a.log b.log`,
+`a.log --out-dir d b.log --count`, and `a.log b.log --count --out-dir d` are equivalent (FR-001, FR-040).
+
+`-h` / `--help` prints usage to stdout and exits 0 (argparse default; not a redaction run).
+
+### Input selection
+
+| Command line | Input read |
+|--------------|------------|
+| no FILE, no `--out-dir` | standard input (FR-002) |
+| one or more FILE | each FILE in order; **standard input is not read** (FR-003) |
+| the same FILE twice | that file is read twice, producing its output twice |
+| no FILE, with `--out-dir` | **nothing** — invalid usage, exit 2 (FR-051) |
+
+### Output destination
+
+Exactly one destination is in effect for a run, chosen before any input is opened.
+
+| Command line | Redacted output goes to | stdout also carries |
+|--------------|------------------------|---------------------|
+| no `--out-dir` | standard output, one combined stream (unchanged) | nothing else |
+| `--out-dir DIR` | `DIR/<final path component of each FILE>`, one file per FILE | exactly one line, `wrote N files` |
+
+---
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Content |
+|--------|---------|
+| stdin | Log text, read only when no FILE is given and `--out-dir` was not given. |
+| stdout | **Without `--out-dir`**: every input line, in input order, exactly once, with matches replaced. Nothing else — no banners, no progress. **With `--out-dir`**: the single line `wrote N files`, and nothing else (FR-047). |
+| stderr | The `--count` summary, or a diagnostic. Nothing on a successful run without `--count` (FR-023). |
+
+**Encoding**: UTF-8 in and out, with undecodable input bytes round-tripped byte-for-byte.
+Behaviour does not vary with `LANG`, `LC_ALL`, or `PYTHONIOENCODING` (FR-005a, FR-005b).
+
+**Line fidelity**: line terminators are preserved exactly, including CRLF and a missing final
+newline. Text outside a match is emitted byte-for-byte (FR-004, FR-005).
+
+**Output files carry exactly this same contract** (FR-044): the bytes in `DIR/app.log` are
+byte-identical to the bytes `python -m logredact app.log` writes to standard output.
+
+**Ordering**: stdout is flushed before anything is written to stderr, so the `--count` summary
+always follows the complete output — and, under `--out-dir`, the confirmation line — even when both
+streams are redirected to the same file (FR-021, FR-052).
+
+---
+
+## The `--count` summary
+
+Exact form, one line, to **stderr**:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N phones=N
+```
+
+- Always all **four** keys, always in this order, single-space separated, `0` when none
+  (FR-037, FR-038). *This replaces the three-key form of v1.*
+- One summary per run, covering all sources together, written once after all output (FR-021).
+- Each `N` equals the number of replacements of that type actually made — equivalently, the
+  number of placeholders of that type present in the output (SC-010).
+- Identical in both destinations: counts cover all FILE arguments together (FR-052).
+- **Suppressed** when a read fails (FR-026, FR-048), when DIR cannot be created (FR-049), when an
+  output file cannot be written (FR-050), on invalid usage (FR-029, FR-051), and when the output
+  consumer closes early (FR-028). In short: a summary appears only on a run that exits 0 (FR-039).
+
+---
+
+## The `wrote N files` confirmation
+
+Exact form, one line, to **stdout**, only on a fully successful `--out-dir` run:
+
+```text
+wrote N files
+```
+
+- N is the number of output files written — **one per FILE argument processed** (FR-046). When two
+  FILE arguments share a name, two writes happen but one file remains, and N counts two.
+- Printed as given, including `wrote 1 files` for a single file. Never pluralised.
+- Written after every FILE has been processed, and it is the **only** thing an `--out-dir` run
+  writes to standard output (FR-047).
+- Never printed without `--out-dir`, and never printed on a run that failed (FR-047).
+- Precedes the `--count` summary on stderr (FR-052).
+
+---
+
+## Exit status
+
+The tool exits with **0 or 2 and no other value** (FR-053, SC-014).
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|--------|-----------|
+| `0` | Every input was read to the end and, under `--out-dir`, every output file was written (FR-024, FR-046); or the output consumer closed early (FR-028). |
+| `2` | A named FILE could not be opened or read (FR-025, FR-048); DIR could not be created (FR-049); an output file could not be opened, written, or closed (FR-050); or the command line was invalid, including `--out-dir` with no FILE (FR-029, FR-051). |
+
+---
+
+## Failure behaviour
+
+All diagnostics take the inherited form `python -m logredact: <path>: <reason>`, name the path
+**exactly as it would be written** — a FILE as typed on the command line, an output path as
+`DIR/name` — and never print a stack trace.
+
+### Unreadable FILE (FR-025, FR-026, FR-027, FR-048)
+
+Covers every reason the input cannot be read as text: it does not exist, permission is denied,
+it is a directory, or reading fails partway through.
+
+- stderr receives a diagnostic naming the FILE as written on the command line and what went wrong.
+- Processing **stops**; later FILE arguments are not opened.
+- Neither the `--count` summary nor `wrote N files` is printed, so an incomplete run is never
+  presented as a complete one.
+- Without `--out-dir`: output already written for earlier files remains on stdout, valid and
+  complete up to the point of failure.
+- With `--out-dir`: output files already completed for earlier FILE arguments **remain valid and
+  complete**, and the output file opened for the failing FILE, if any, is removed (see below).
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### DIR cannot be created (FR-049)
+
+For example its parent is a regular file, or is not writable.
+
+- stderr receives a diagnostic naming DIR and what went wrong.
+- **No input is read** — DIR is created before the first FILE is opened (FR-045).
+- stdout is empty; no summary.
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### Output file cannot be opened, written, or closed (FR-050)
+
+For example the output name already exists as a directory, or the disk is full.
+
+- stderr receives a diagnostic naming the **output path** and what went wrong.
+- Processing stops; later FILE arguments are not opened.
+- stdout is empty; no `wrote N files`, no summary.
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### The partial-output guarantee (FR-050a)
+
+When a run fails after an output file has been opened but before it has been completely written —
+whether the failure was reading the input or writing the output — that file is **deleted** before
+the tool exits.
+
+- After any failed `--out-dir` run, every file remaining in DIR that this run wrote is a complete
+  redaction of its input. DIR never holds a truncated file under a legitimate name (SC-013a).
+- A file of that name that existed in DIR **before** the run is gone with it. Its previous contents
+  are not restored — opening the output replaced them.
+- A FILE that could not be opened at all never had an output file created, so a pre-existing file
+  of its output name is left untouched.
+- If the deletion itself fails, that is reported on stderr as an **additional** diagnostic and the
+  exit status is still `2`. The tool does not retry and does not fall silent.
+
+### Invalid command line (FR-029, FR-051)
+
+```text
+usage: python -m logredact [-h] [--count] [--out-dir DIR] [files ...]
+python -m logredact: error: unrecognized arguments: --redact-all
+```
+
+- stderr receives a usage message naming the offending argument, or — for `--out-dir` with no FILE
+  — stating that `--out-dir` requires at least one FILE.
+- No input is read, **stdout is empty**, no summary, and **DIR is not created**.
+- The unknown argument is never silently ignored nor reinterpreted as a filename; `--out-dir` is
+  never silently ignored.
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### Output consumer closes early (FR-028)
+
+When stdout is closed before the tool finishes writing (e.g. `| head -5`):
+
+- Processing stops immediately.
+- **Nothing** is written to stderr: no diagnostic, no `BrokenPipeError` trace, no summary.
+- Exit status `0`.
+- Under `--out-dir` the only thing written to stdout is the confirmation line, so this path is
+  reached only at the very end, after every output file is complete.
+
+---
+
+## Caveat: DIR containing an input FILE
+
+Pointing `--out-dir` at a directory that holds one of the FILE arguments **replaces that input
+file**, because an input file sitting in DIR is exactly the "existing file of that name" FR-043
+says to replace. The tool does no same-path detection. The resulting contents of such a file are
+not part of this contract.
+
+---
+
+## Worked examples
+
+Each is directly executable; see [`../quickstart.md`](../quickstart.md). All were verified against
+the Phase 0 prototype.
+
+| # | Command | stdout | stderr | Status |
+|---|---------|--------|--------|--------|
+| 1 | `echo 'call +14155552671 now' \| python -m logredact` | `call [PHONE] now` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 2 | `echo 'a@b.io +442071838750' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL] [PHONE]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=1` | 0 |
+| 3 | `echo '+1234567' \| python -m logredact` | `+1234567` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 4 | `echo '+1234567890123456' \| python -m logredact` | `+1234567890123456` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 5 | `echo '+4111111111111111' \| python -m logredact --count` | `+4111111111111111` | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0` | 0 |
+| 6 | `echo '+12345678@example.com' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0 phones=0` | 0 |
+| 7 | `echo 'build v2+12345678 ok' \| python -m logredact` | `build v2[PHONE] ok` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 8 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean app.log web.log` | `wrote 2 files` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 9 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean var/logs/app.log` | `wrote 1 files` (written to `clean/app.log`) | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 10 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean --count app.log` | `wrote 1 files` | `emails=… phones=…` | 0 |
+| 11 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean good.log missing.log later.log` | *(empty)* — `clean/good.log` complete, `later.log` never opened | names `missing.log` | 2 |
+| 12 | `python -m logredact --out-dir blocker/sub a.log` (`blocker` is a file) | *(empty)* — nothing read | names `blocker/sub` | 2 |
+| 13 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean a.log` (`clean/a.log` is a directory) | *(empty)* | names `clean/a.log` | 2 |
+| 14 | `python -m logredact --out-dir clean` | *(empty)* — `clean/` not created | usage: `--out-dir` requires at least one FILE | 2 |
+| 15 | `python -m logredact --redact-all` | *(empty)* | usage naming `--redact-all` | 2 |
+
+---
+
+## Stability
+
+The following are part of the contract and may not change without a spec amendment: the four
+placeholder strings, the summary format and its **four** key names, the `wrote N files` form, the
+`{0, 2}` exit-status set, the stream assignment of output vs. confirmation vs. diagnostics, the
+UTF-8 round-trip guarantee, and the partial-output guarantee.
+
+## Internal API (not a public contract)
+
+`src/logredact/` exposes nothing to third parties; only `python -m logredact` is public. The module
+split described in `plan.md` exists for testability (Principle III) and may be reorganised freely
+as long as this contract holds.
