+# Contract: redaction rules (amendment)
+
+**Feature**: `002-redact-phones-out-dir` | **Date**: 2026-11-14
+
+This amends
+[`../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/redaction-rules.md`](../../001-redact-log-pii/contracts/redaction-rules.md).
+Two changes, and nothing else:
+
+1. A fourth recognition rule, **phone number** → `[PHONE]` (FR-030 … FR-036).
+2. A **narrowing** of the payment-card rule: a digit run immediately preceded by `+` is never a
+   card candidate (FR-032a).
+
+The email and IPv4 rules are untouched. Every example below was verified against the Phase 0
+prototype (research.md R-010, R-011, R-012).
+
+---
+
+## The scanning algorithm — unchanged in shape
+
+One compiled alternation, scanned once per line, left to right, by `re.sub` with a callback:
+
+```text
+(?P<email>…)|(?P<ip>…)|(?P<card>…)|(?P<phone>\+[0-9]{8,15}(?![0-9]))
+```
+
+- **Leftmost wins** (FR-018). A candidate that starts earlier beats one that starts later, however
+  long the later one is.
+- **Branch order breaks a tie** at the same start offset (FR-036):
+  `email → ip → card → phone`. Phone is last; appending it leaves the inherited three-way order
+  untouched.
+- **No re-scanning** (FR-035, FR-020). Scanning resumes after a replaced span, so the digits inside
+  a `[PHONE]` are never re-examined by the card or IPv4 rule and cannot be counted twice. A
+  *rejected* candidate also consumes its span: it is returned whole and unchanged, and the scan
+  resumes after it.
+- A candidate that fails validation increments no counter. Only a replacement counts.
+
+In practice only `email` and `phone` can begin at the same offset, because `+` is in the email
+local-part class while `ip` and `card` candidates begin with a digit.
+
+---
+
+## Rule: phone number — new
+
+| Property | Value |
+|----------|-------|
+| Pattern | `\+[0-9]{8,15}(?![0-9])` |
+| Placeholder | `[PHONE]` |
+| Counter | `phones` |
+| Precedence | 4th (last) |
+| Validation after the match | none — the pattern alone decides |
+
+### What the pattern's three parts mean
+
+| Part | Requirement it carries |
+|------|------------------------|
+| `\+` | The `+` is **part of the match** and is replaced with the digits, so `+14155552671` becomes `[PHONE]` with no leading `+` left behind (FR-031). |
+| `[0-9]{8,15}` | 8 to 15 **ASCII** digits, no separators of any kind. Other scripts' decimal digits do not count (FR-031, FR-033). |
+| `(?![0-9])` | A run followed by another ASCII digit is rejected **whole** — never truncated to its first 15 digits, never partially redacted (FR-032). |
+| *(no lookbehind)* | There is no left-hand guard. Whatever precedes the `+` — a letter, a digit, punctuation, another `+`, or the start of the line — the candidate matches (FR-031a). This differs deliberately from the adjacency guards on the IPv4 and card rules. |
+| *(nothing after)* | Punctuation or any other non-digit immediately after the digits is not consumed (FR-034). |
+
+### Recognition table
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `+14155552671` | `[PHONE]` | 11 digits |
+| `+12345678` | `[PHONE]` | 8 digits — the minimum |
+| `+455673758689985` | `[PHONE]` | 15 digits — the maximum |
+| `+1234567` | *unchanged* | 7 digits — below the minimum (FR-033) |
+| `+1234567890123456` | *unchanged* | 16 digits — rejected whole, not truncated (FR-032) |
+| `+14155552671.` | `[PHONE].` | the full stop is not consumed (FR-034) |
+| `(+14155552671)` | `([PHONE])` | brackets preserved |
+| `v2+12345678` | `v2[PHONE]` | no left-hand guard (FR-031a) |
+| `x=99+12345678` | `x=99[PHONE]` | likewise — accepted over-redaction |
+| `+12345678` at line start | `[PHONE]` | likewise |
+| `++12345678` | `+[PHONE]` | the second `+` starts the candidate |
+| `+14155552671+442071838750` | `[PHONE][PHONE]` | the first match ends at the `+` that starts the second |
+| `+` alone, `+abc` | *unchanged* | no digits follow (FR-033) |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | *unchanged* | separators are not international format; see the card note below |
+| `14155552671` | *unchanged* | no `+`; falls to the card rule, which rejects it as too short |
+| `+١٤١٥٥٥٥٢٦٧١` | *unchanged* | non-ASCII digits never count (FR-031) |
+| `+1415555٢٦٧١` | *unchanged* | only 7 ASCII digits; and because the run is cut short by a non-ASCII character rather than a digit, FR-032's rejection does not fire either |
+| `[PHONE]` already in the input | *unchanged, uncounted* | nothing matches it (FR-020) |
+
+### Interaction with the other rules
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `+12345678@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | email and phone both start at the `+`; email wins the tie and is what is counted (FR-036) |
+| `jane+orders@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | the email candidate starts earlier; the `+` is not followed by 8 digits anyway |
+| `a@b.io +442071838750` | `[EMAIL] [PHONE]` | two separate candidates |
+| `+14155552671 10.0.0.7` | `[PHONE] [IP]` | two separate candidates |
+| `+` then 13–15 Luhn-valid digits | `[PHONE]`, counted once as a phone | the phone candidate starts one character earlier than the card candidate, so leftmost-wins settles it; the digits are never re-examined (FR-035) |
+| `+` then 16–19 Luhn-valid digits | *unchanged* | too long for a phone (FR-032) **and** not a card (FR-032a) |
+
+---
+
+## Rule: payment card — narrowed
+
+The pattern, the 13–19-digit bound, and the Luhn check are all unchanged. One condition is added:
+
+> A card candidate whose first character is immediately preceded by a literal `+` is **rejected**
+> and returned whole and unchanged, whether or not a phone match was made at that `+` (FR-032a).
+
+A leading `+` marks the run as telephone-shaped: the 8-to-15 bound then decides whether it becomes
+`[PHONE]`, and a run outside that bound is simply left alone.
+
+| Input | v1 output | v2 output | Why |
+|-------|-----------|-----------|-----|
+| `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` | `[CARD]` | unchanged — no leading `+` |
+| `+4111111111111111` | `+[CARD]` | *unchanged* | 16 digits: too long for a phone, and the `+` takes it out of the card rule |
+| `+4111 1111 1111 1111` | `+[CARD]` | *unchanged* | likewise, with separators |
+| `+455673758689985` | `+[CARD]` | `[PHONE]` | 15 digits: the phone rule claims it, starting at the `+` |
+| `+1 415 555 2671` | *unchanged* | *unchanged* | the candidate beginning after the `+` is rejected whole; nothing inside it is re-scanned |
+
+This is a **suppression only** — strictly fewer card matches than v1, never more. The rejection is
+applied to the whole maximal candidate (not by excluding the first digit from the pattern), which
+is what keeps it from being an addition: excluding the first digit would let the scanner restart
+inside the run and find a shorter Luhn-valid tail, producing `[CARD]` where v1 produced nothing.
+Verified; see research.md R-012.
+
+**Accepted cost, recorded in the spec**: a real card number written with a stray leading `+` is no
+longer redacted.
+
+---
+
+## Known deviation (inherited, deliberately not fixed here)
+
+FR-031 states that non-ASCII decimal digits are left unchanged "exactly as the existing IPv4 and
+payment-card rules are read". That is not true of the existing rules: their patterns use `\d`,
+which matches every Unicode decimal digit, and `int()` accepts those digits, so a 13-to-19-digit
+Arabic-Indic run that passes Luhn is redacted as `[CARD]` — and was before this feature.
+
+The new phone rule uses `[0-9]` and is correct. The inherited patterns are **left as they are**,
+because such an input contains neither a phone number nor a `+`-prefixed digit run, and FR-054
+requires its output to be byte-identical to the pre-change tool's. Recorded as spec defect D-2 in
+[`../plan.md`](../plan.md) for a separate decision.
